//RS /// A cryptographic random draw is intentionally not replayed by a
//RS /// deterministic world. Route the canonical AOT Core entry through this
//RS /// boundary so it cannot silently fall back to operating-system entropy.
//RS /// A missing or rejected provider is `None`; the Jet wrapper in
//RS /// `Core/crypto/random.jet` reports it as `CryptoError.Unavailable`.
//RS fn jet_std_crypto_random_bytes_controlled(n: i64) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
//RS     jet_scheduler_world_reject_uncontrolled("entropy");
//RS     jet_crypto_entropy_bytes(n).ok()
//RS }
//RS 
//RS /// The same classification, on the one thread that is NOT the program's entry
//RS /// boundary: the interrupt dispatcher.
//RS ///
//RS /// A Jet interrupt handler runs on the dispatcher thread — the count and the
//RS /// drain rule belong to `Prelude/CoreLib/Top/Interrupt.rs`, the thread and the
//RS /// `Arc<dyn Fn>` storage to that file's sibling `jet_os_interrupt` adapter in
//RS /// `Prelude/CoreLib/Top/FSIoEnvOsTesting.rs` — so every control transfer a
//RS /// handler raises unwinds into that thread's `catch_unwind` and can never reach
//RS /// `jet_runtime_boundary` above. Handing those payloads straight to the
//RS /// scheduler's loose-panic reporter, which knows only deadline unwinds and
//RS /// panic text, dropped both meanings a handler can carry: `process.exit`
//RS /// stopped ending the process (a signalled program then ran forever with its
//RS /// handlers already done) and a handler's stop printed no report at all.
//RS ///
//RS /// Two of the terminal rules differ from the entry boundary's, on purpose. An
//RS /// explicit exit still ends the process: that is what the program asked for,
//RS /// and which thread asked is not part of the request. A stop only *reports*
//RS /// here: the drain still owes every later handler its turn in registration
//RS /// order, so one failing handler must not cancel the ones registered after it.
//RS ///
//RS /// `report_other` is the tail of that chain, and it is a parameter for a
//RS /// structural reason rather than a stylistic one. This file is embedded in
//RS /// EVERY generated program; the payloads outside the list above belong to parts
//RS /// that are not. A blown deadline is `Prelude/Scheduler.rs`'s
//RS /// `JetDeadlineUnwind`, reported by that part's `jet_report_caught_unwind`, and
//RS /// the scheduler ships only when a program reaches it
//RS /// (`Codegen/mod.rs::push_core_runtime`) — never for `Codegen/mod.rs::emit`,
//RS /// `emit_tests`, or the wasm module. So the adapter, the one place that has both
//RS /// parts in scope, names the tail; this file names nothing it does not own, and
//RS /// a program with no scheduler cannot inherit a dangling reference to one (I2).
//RS /// The tail is a required argument, not a leftover payload handed back to a
